  "worked_solution": "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ and passes through the point $(-3,4).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+2+4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =6-3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{18}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4)$ is $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$",
